          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best protein powder for men?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most men, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey: about 24 g of protein per scoop, good value per gram and Informed Choice certification, per the brand. Large lifters can use two scoops for a 40 g-plus dose. For cutting, an isolate such as Dymatize ISO100 gives more protein per calorie, and Garden of Life Sport suits dairy-free men.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do men need a different protein powder than women?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Protein is digested and used the same way in every adult. Men tend to be heavier and carry more muscle, so per-kilogram guidelines produce bigger totals and bigger per-meal doses. The right product depends on your goal, diet and budget. What changes with size is whether you need one scoop or two.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many grams of protein should a man have in a shake?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A useful guide is about 0.4 g per kg of body weight per meal. For a 75 kg man that is about 30 g; for a 100 kg man, about 40 g. Research suggests 40 g after heavy whole-body training can stimulate more muscle building than 20 g, so larger lifters can reasonably double their scoop.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does soy protein lower testosterone?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not at normal intakes, according to the evidence. A 2010 meta-analysis and later reviews found that soy protein and isoflavones did not meaningfully alter testosterone in men. Case reports of hormonal effects involve extreme amounts of soy far beyond a daily shake. Soy is a complete protein and a reasonable choice for men.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are testosterone-boosting protein powders worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Ingredients such as tribulus, fenugreek and D-aspartic acid have weak or inconsistent evidence in healthy men, and they are often hidden in proprietary blends. Products sold for male performance also feature in FDA warnings about undeclared ingredients. Buy a plain, certified protein and see a doctor if you suspect low testosterone.
